Spec: spmpcfg register format widened from 16-bit to SXLEN-bit; previously undefined upper bits risk information leakage if implementations don't zero-extend and if software doesn't mask properly on read-modify-write
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:348-349  
      CSR_SISELECT (0x150) and CSR_SIREG (0x151) are pure CSR address constants for the AIA indirect register access window. These definitions have no bearing on spmpcfg — they refer to a completely different architectural register (S-mode indirect register select for AIA, not SPMP configuration). No security issue.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/csr.h:524-525  
      CSR_ISELECT and CSR_IREG are legacy-name aliases for CSR_SISELECT and CSR_SIREG respectively. These are simple #define macros that redirect to the S-mode AIA indirect register select addresses. No interaction with spmpcfg.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/asm/kvm_aia.h:52-53  kvm_vcpu_aia_csr
      vsiselect is declared as 'unsigned long' (SXLEN bits) with no masking applied on reads or writes within the struct itself. When kvm_riscv_vcpu_aia_put() reads CSR_VSISELECT via csr_read(), it captures the full SXLEN-bit value including any architecturally-reserved upper bits beyond ISELECT_MASK (bits 8:0). These unmasked bits then flow to userspace via KVM_GET_ONE_REG through the kvm_riscv_aia_csr UAPI struct. This is the same vulnerability pattern as the spmpcfg issue: architecturally-undefined upper bits of a register are exposed to less-privileged software without zeroing. The RISC-V AIA spec defines only bits [8:0] as the ISELECT field; bits [SXLEN-1:9] are reserved and should read as zero.
  [OK] arch/riscv/include/asm/kvm_aia.h:147-149  
      The KVM_RISCV_VCPU_AIA_CSR_FUNCS table entry maps CSR_SIREG to kvm_riscv_vcpu_aia_rmw_ireg, which properly applies ISELECT_MASK (GENMASK(8,0)) when reading VSISELECT for functional dispatch (aia.c:399). This dispatch mechanism itself does not leak unmasked bits; the masking is applied in the rmw handler before the value is used for indirect register routing.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/include/uapi/asm/kvm.h:89-91  kvm_riscv_aia_csr
      The siselect field in the UAPI struct kvm_riscv_aia_csr is declared as 'unsigned long' with no semantic constraint documentation. This is the userspace-facing type that carries the full SXLEN-bit value read from CSR_VSISELECT by kvm_riscv_vcpu_aia_put(). There is no masking or zeroing of reserved upper bits (ISELECT_MASK only covers bits [8:0]) at the KVM boundary between kernel and userspace. Any implementation that does not zero the reserved bits of siselect will leak them to the VMM.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia.c:116-144  kvm_riscv_vcpu_aia_load
      This function writes csr->vsiselect to the hardware CSR_VSISELECT register. Writing the full unsigned long value is architecturally fine because reserved bits in the hardware register are ignored on writes (write-ignore for reserved fields). The risk is on the read side (kvm_riscv_vcpu_aia_put), not the write side.
  [RISK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia.c:146-179  kvm_riscv_vcpu_aia_put
      This function reads CSR_VSISELECT via csr_read() (line 169) and stores the full unsigned long result into csr->vsiselect without masking the architecturally-reserved upper bits (ISELECT_MASK covers only bits [8:0]). This means on implementations where the reserved bits of VSISELECT are not hardware-zeroed, any microarchitectural state in those upper bits is captured and subsequently exposed to userspace via KVM_GET_ONE_REG. This is the exact vulnerability pattern described in the security spec, albeit for VSISELECT rather than spmpcfg.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia_imsic.c:372-420  imsic_vsfile_local_read
